codex
The local construction is sound as an inverse generator, but I found an important scope nuance: Section 3.2 uses degree-≤1 polynomials when \((k,\ell)=(6,2)\); the high-degree trinomial is a benchmark encoding of an independent set, not itself one of the paper’s line vertices. I’m treating this as a structured restriction of the paper’s fixed-core affine-line construction, not as evidence for its random extremal distribution. The local gates pass; I’m now running the required four-vendor loop to see whether the Track B family actually survives.
Full-text review confirms the native fixed-core affine-line construction in Sections 3.2–3.3. An existing Track B inverse generator is present; its local correctness and attack measurements pass, with an explicit caveat that its trinomial restriction is benchmark-designed rather than the paper’s random U distribution.
